    {
      "parameters": {
        "method": "POST",
        "url": "http://host.docker.internal:11434/api/chat",
        "sendBody": true,
        "specifyBody": "json",
        "jsonBody": "={\n  \"model\": \"llama3.2:3b\",\n  \"stream\": false,\n  \"format\": \"json\",\n  \"messages\": [\n    {\n      \"role\": \"system\",\n      \"content\": \"You are a strict B2B lead qualification analyst for an AI automation freelancer. Your job is to determine whether a company is a genuinely promising prospect for AI and workflow automation services. IMPORTANT SCORING RULES: Use ONLY facts explicitly provided in the lead data. Never invent pain points, business processes, buying signals, or company information. Do not assume that a company needs automation simply because it is large or belongs to a technology-related industry. Lack of evidence must reduce the score. Be conservative. A normal company with no explicit automation need should NOT receive 80+. HIGH should be difficult to achieve. Score these five factors: industry_fit 0-20, company_fit 0-15, automation_potential 0-25, pain_point_strength 0-20, buying_intent 0-20. For industry_fit, strong relevance to AI or workflow automation means a higher score, while weak or unrelated relevance means a lower score. For company_fit, consider company size and available company information, but do not assume large companies automatically need automation. For automation_potential, give a high score ONLY when the provided information indicates processes that could realistically benefit from automation. If no automation-related evidence is provided, keep this score low. For pain_point_strength, give a high score ONLY when explicit pain points are provided. If no pain points are provided, score this low. Do NOT invent pain points. For buying_intent, give a high score ONLY when explicit buying intent, a request for help, or a clear purchase signal is provided. If there is no buying signal, score this low. The total score MUST equal industry_fit + company_fit + automation_potential + pain_point_strength + buying_intent. Classification: 80-100 = HIGH, 60-79 = MEDIUM, 0-59 = LOW. For pain_points, include ONLY pain points explicitly supported by the input. If none are provided, return an empty array []. Return ONLY valid JSON. Do NOT use markdown. Do NOT include explanations outside the JSON. Do NOT include text before or after the JSON. Return exactly these fields: score, tier, industry_fit, company_fit, automation_potential, pain_point_strength, buying_intent, pain_points, recommended_service, outreach_angle, reason.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"role\": \"user\",\n      \"content\": \"Analyze this lead:\\\\nName: {{$json.name}}\\\\nEmail: {{$json.email}}\\\\nCompany: {{$json.company}}\\\\nWebsite: {{$json.website}}\\\\nIndustry: {{$json.industry}}\\\\nCountry: {{$json.country}}\\\\nEmployees: {{$json.employees}}\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "options": {}
      },
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.httpRequest",
      "typeVersion": 4.4,
      "position": [
        1248,
        16
      ],
      "id": "aa573247-32a6-4bfd-83b1-1d9f6833dd2e",
      "name": "HTTP Request1"
    },
    {
      "parameters": {
        "jsCode": "const results = [];\n\nfor (const item of $input.all()) {\n  const content = item.json.message.content;\n\n  const parsed = JSON.parse(content);\n\n  results.push({\n    json: {\n      ...item.json,\n      ...parsed\n    }\n  });\n}\n\nreturn results;"
      },
